#{[17592186552071 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 19 \\\\ 3 & 25 \\\\ 4 & 31 \\\\ 5 & 40\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552079 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552087 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552088 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -10 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 14 \\\\ 1 & 24\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552093 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552101 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 31\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552107 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552111 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 32 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 42 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 15\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552112 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552113 :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552114 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552124 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 7 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 10 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552140 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552147 :answer/value "A\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552149 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552152 :answer/value "A\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552158 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552163 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552178 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552183 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552195 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552206 :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552209 :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552224 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552225 :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552233 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552235 :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552246 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}15 & 31\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552249 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552250 :answer/value "$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552252 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A"] [17592186552254 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552405 :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-8{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=9\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-9.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-8I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}-8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-8I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-9)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -8\\end{bmatrix}+9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A+9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{17},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186552410 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$30$** | **$25$** | **$20$** | **$15$** | **$12$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$2$** | **$6$** | **$12$** | **$20$** | **$30$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the hyperplane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.  Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?"] [17592186552410 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$30$** | **$25$** | **$20$** | **$15$** | **$12$** |\n| ${x}_{3}$ | **$2$** | **$6$** | **$12$** | **$20$** | **$30$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-6$ | $0$ | $4$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the hyperplane  \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}_{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 30 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 25 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 20 & 12 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 15 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 12 & 30\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186552411 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same  elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:=6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186552418 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$5$** | **$9$** | **$15$** | **$22$** | **$28$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $14$ | $24$ |\n\n \n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?"] [17592186552418 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$5$** | **$9$** | **$15$** | **$22$** | **$28$** |\n| $y$ | $-10$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $14$ | $24$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 15 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 28\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 14 \\\\ 24\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186552419 :question/problem "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $15{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -15\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186552419 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $$\n\\displaystyle [x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\cdot 4+(-15)\\cdot 3}{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40-45}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{25} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n15{x}_{2}=15\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)=-3.\n$$"] [17592186552421 :question/problem "Consider the matrix $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and the quadratic curve ${x}^{T}\\,Ax=1.$ Given that $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=9$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ find the principal axes of the curve."] [17592186552421 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $A$ is symmetric. \nThe principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=1\n$$\n\nare the vectors that give an orthonormal eigenvector basis for $A.$ Let's now find these eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-9\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186552422 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [17592186552422 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of the identity matrix and adding $-9$ times the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-9){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-9{R}_{2}$ to $A.$"] [17592186552432 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the  three given elementary row operations. \n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{5}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186552433 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $$\n\\displaystyle L(x)={(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)\n$$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186552433 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b)=\\frac{1}{2}A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{1}{2}A)}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & ={A}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}Ax-b) \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 34\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}62 \\\\ 45\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552440 :question/worked-solution "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2}$  to $A.$ \n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{3}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186552443 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the two given elementary row operations\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}.$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}$ The  corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186552450 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [17592186552450 :question/worked-solution "The matrix $E$ is obtained by taking the first row of the identity matrix and adding $6$ times the second row:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+6{R}_{2}$ to $A.$"] [17592186552453 :question/problem "Let $$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find the row operation that, when applied to a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ gives the result of the product $EA.$"] [17592186552453 :question/worked-solution "The given matrix $E$ is obtained by multiplying the second row of the identity matrix by $-2$: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $EA$ is equivalent to applying the row operation  ${R}_{2}:=-2{R}_{2}$ to $A.$"] [17592186552454 :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=14{x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=15\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=5.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-15I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-15\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -9\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-15I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-5\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186552468 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$19$** | **$25$** | **$31$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $9$ | $13$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186552468 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$15$** | **$19$** | **$25$** | **$31$** | **$40$** |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $9$ | $13$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 15 \\\\ 2 & 19 \\\\ 3 & 25 \\\\ 4 & 31 \\\\ 5 & 40\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 27\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186552476 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186552476 :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{T}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y}\\right)}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}52 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552487 :question/worked-solution "Consider the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ To determine the curve defined by the given equation, we reduce $Q(x)$ to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of $Q(x),$ which is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda  & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =2,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our quadratic form can be reduced to $2{y}_{1}^{2}.$ \n\nIn the new variables, the given curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the two parallel lines ${y}_{1}=\\sqrt{3}$ and ${y}_{1}=-\\sqrt{3}.$"] [17592186552489 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186552489 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4+0-3 \\\\ -3+0+2 \\\\ -3+0+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552496 :question/worked-solution "We need to find an elementary matrix $E$ such that $EA$ is equivalent to applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}$ to $A.$ \n\nTo find $E,$ we apply the given row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =E\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equivalent notation in terms of matrix multiplication is $EA:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186552500 :question/problem "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the plane $y={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using multiple linear regression as \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?"] [17592186552500 :question/worked-solution "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** | **$10$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$10$** | **$7$** | **$5$** | **$3$** | **$2$** |\n| $y$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 7 \\\\ 6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 3 \\\\ 10 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector."] [17592186552502 :question/problem "Compute the gradient of the function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R$ defined by\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{T}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x\n$$\n\nwith respect to $x$ at  $$\n\\displaystyle x={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}.\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"30px\">$\\,{∇}_{x}f=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186552502 :question/worked-solution "Defining the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe have $f(x)={x}^{T}Ax.$\n\nSince $A$ is not symmetric, we use the more general formula for the gradient of a quadratic function:\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}f(x)=({A}^{T}+A)x\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}+A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, compute the gradient at $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}:\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f(x) & =({A}^{T}+A)x \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+0+0 \\\\ 0+0+0 \\\\ 1+0+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552513 :question/worked-solution "First, consider the quadratic form $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}+24{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-5{x}_{2}^{2}.$ The matrix of the form is\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is symmetric. The principal axes of the curve \n\n$$\n{x}^{T}\\,Ax=52\n$$\n\nare the vectors that form an orthonormal eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\nBy solving the characteristic equation $|A-\\lambda I|=0,$ we obtain\n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=13\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-13.$\n\nLet's now find the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-13I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-13\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-13,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-13)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 12 \\\\ 12 & -5\\end{bmatrix}+13\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-13I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our curve are\n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186552525 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the elementary matrices corresponding to the three given elementary row operations.\n\n- The first elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second elementary row operation is ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third elementary row operation is ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2}.$ The corresponding elementary matrix can be found by applying this same elementary row operation to the identity matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={E}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo apply the first elementary row operation to the matrix $A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{1}$ on the left and get ${E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\nThen, to apply the second elementary row operation to ${E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{2}$ on the left and get ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, to apply the third elementary row operation to ${E}_{2}{E}_{1}A,$ we multiply by ${E}_{3}$ on the left and get ${E}_{3}{E}_{2}{E}_{1}A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot A\n$$"] [17592186552753 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =8i+6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨8,6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨8,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8+3\\cdot 6+(-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552766 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-5)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =9i+j-7k \\\\  & =⟨9,1,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨4,-3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨9,1,-7⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 9+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552770 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-5)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552775 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-4)\\cdot 5)i-((-3)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot (-4))j+((-3)\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =24i-24j-12k \\\\  & =⟨24,-24,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨4,2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨24,-24,-12⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 24+2\\cdot (-24)+(-4)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =96-48+48 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552776 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3))i-(1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-3))j+(1\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-11i-17j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-11,-17,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-5,-5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-11,-17,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-11)+(-5)\\cdot (-17)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =55+85+2 \\\\  & =142\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186552875 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552892 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}-8 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186552901 :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552902 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -11\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552904 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552919 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -2\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186552925 :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552929 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -110 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552944 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552950 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186552980 :answer/value "\\frac{\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{vmatrix}}{\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}}"] [17592186552986 :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186552990 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553018 :answer/value "$U=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186553032 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 10 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553044 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553049 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u"] [17592186553053 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553061 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553062 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553077 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 110 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553103 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -110 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553105 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553110 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553113 :answer/value "{u}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}u"] [17592186553119 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186553125 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"]}
